---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro'
import Nav from '../components/Nav.astro'
import Footer from '../components/Footer.astro'

// Map each video to its title + script when matched. Leave title empty until matched.
const videos = [
  { id: '07f7b5', file: 'Captions_07F7B5', title: 'The $4 Soda', script: `A four-dollar soda feels totally fine on a beach vacation, and outrageous from your own fridge. Same soda, same four dollars. The only thing that changed is what it sat next to.

People can't judge a price on its own. They judge it against whatever's nearby. And your price isn't being weighed on its merits either. It's being weighed against the cheap guy's quote, a DIY video, a "my brother-in-law could probably do it." You can't see what's sitting next to you in their head, but it's setting your price for you. More leads won't fix that. Each one is comparing you to something you can't see.

You're not short on leads. You're short on calls. So what's sitting next to your price in your prospect's mind right now?` },
  { id: '186e2b', file: 'Captions_186E2B', title: '15% vs 99%', script: `Two countries, side by side. In one, fifteen percent of people are organ donors. In the other, ninety-nine percent. Same people, same values. The only difference? One country's form makes you check a box to opt in. The other makes you opt out. That's it. One box swung it from fifteen to ninety-nine.

Now look at your leads. They wanted to call. They meant to. Then something, a form, a wait, a step you can't even see, stopped them. And you'll never know which step, or how many it cost you. More leads won't fix that. You'd lose them at the same invisible box.

You're not short on leads. You're short on calls. So what's the box sitting between "interested" and "called," and would you even know it was there?` },
  { id: '41465c', file: 'Captions_41465C', title: 'One Name Beats a Million', script: `"A million children are in need." Versus: "This is Peter. He's hungry. Here's his photo." The second one raises far more money, every single time. Stalin put it coldly: one death is a tragedy, a million is a statistic. Numbers inform. One specific person moves.

Your reviews say "great service, highly recommend, 4.9 stars." That's a statistic. The prospect scrolls right past it. What actually stops them is one specific story: one named person, one real job, one problem solved, that sounds like them. More leads won't fix that. They all scroll past the same blur of stars.

You're not short on leads. You're short on calls. So does your proof have a face, or just an average?` },
  { id: 'af561e', file: 'Captions_AF561E', title: '24 Jars', script: `A store set out twenty-four jars of jam to sample. Big crowd, lots of interest. Three percent bought. The next week, six jars. Thirty percent bought. Same jam, same shoppers. Ten times the sales, from fewer choices.

Here's the part nobody expects. The lost sales weren't people who tasted and said no. They were people who couldn't decide, so they decided nothing. More options didn't help them choose. It froze them.

Your leads are standing at that twenty-four-jar table. You, three other contractors, the guy their neighbor mentioned, the one with more reviews. So they don't pick wrong. They just don't pick. More leads won't fix that. You'd add more people to a table that's already too crowded to choose from.

You're not short on leads. You're short on calls. So how many people wanted to hire you, and hired no one at all?` },
  { id: 'b1d9bc', file: 'Captions_B1D9BC', title: "They're Not Deciding. They're Scared.", script: `Harry Beckwith spent a career selling things you can't touch: services, promises. And his finding was this: when a buyer stalls, it's almost never that they need more information. It's fear. He put it perfectly. At that point you don't need to put more sale in, you need to take some of the fear out.

A homeowner hiring you is buying a promise that a stranger will show up, do invisible work behind a wall, and not wreck their house. That's a scary thing to say yes to. So they don't say no. They just go quiet. More leads won't fix that. You'd just have more scared people going quiet.

You're not short on leads. You're short on calls. So how many of your "maybes" were really just afraid, and never told you?` },
  { id: 'b8dd4c', file: 'Captions_B8DD4C', title: 'Justice Runs on Lunch', script: `Researchers tracked one judge's parole decisions across a full day. Right after a meal break, he approved about two out of three. Right before lunch, near zero. Same kinds of cases, same judge. The only thing that changed was how tired and hungry he was.

Your prospect decides about you somewhere in their day too. Maybe at 4:51 in the afternoon, between a problem at work and a kid to pick up. And when people are worn down, they don't make the brave choice. They make the easy one: not yet. More leads won't fix that. They each decide on a full or empty tank you can't see.

You're not short on leads. You're short on calls. So what mood was your last lost lead in when they decided not to call?` },
  { id: 'c317bc', file: 'Captions_C317BC', title: "The Number That Wasn't Yours", script: `Real estate agents, trained professionals, toured the exact same house. Half were told it listed at a hundred forty-nine thousand. Half were told a hundred nineteen. The first group valued it sixteen thousand dollars higher. Same house, same experts. One number, planted early, moved everyone. And they swore it didn't.

Your prospect got anchored before they ever called you. They saw a price somewhere: a lowball competitor, a Google estimate, a buddy's "should run you about." And that became the ruler they measure you with. You never saw it. You just felt the "you're too expensive" you never earned. More leads won't fix that. They all show up holding a price you didn't set.

You're not short on leads. You're short on calls. So whose number are your prospects measuring you against, one you've never even seen?` },
  { id: 'c51ded', file: 'Captions_C51DED', title: 'Five Minutes', script: `Researchers tracked thousands of sales leads. Reach out within five minutes, and you're twenty-one times more likely to ever qualify that lead than if you wait thirty. Same lead. The only variable was minutes.

And here's the brutal part. The leads you lose this way are silent. They filled out the form, didn't hear back fast, called the next guy, and you have no record they ever existed. They don't show up as a loss. They just never show up at all. More leads won't fix that. You'd just have more people timing out in the gap.

You're not short on leads. You're short on calls. So how many people tried to give you money, before you ever got back to them?` },
]
---
<Base
  title="Watch — You're Not Short on Leads. You're Short on Calls. | EngageEngine™"
  description="Short films on why interested buyers never become phone calls. The behavioral science behind the calls you never received."
>
  <Nav />
  <main>

    <section class="vh-hero">
      <div class="vh-wrap">
        <span class="vh-eyebrow">The Series</span>
        <h1>You're not short on leads.<br />You're short on calls.</h1>
        <p class="vh-sub">Short films on the invisible steps where interested buyers stop before they ever dial. Each one is built on a real study of how people actually decide.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="vh-grid-section">
      <div class="vh-wrap">
        <div class="vh-grid">
          {videos.map((v, i) => (
            <article class="vh-card">
              <div class="vh-player" data-src={`/video/hub/v-${v.id}.mp4`}>
                <video
                  poster={`/video/hub/v-${v.id}-poster.jpg`}
                  preload="none"
                  playsinline
                  controls
                >
                  <source src={`/video/hub/v-${v.id}.mp4`} type="video/mp4" />
                </video>
              </div>
              <div class="vh-meta">
                <h3>{v.title || `Video ${i + 1}`}</h3>
                {!v.title && <p class="vh-file">{v.file}</p>}
                {v.script && (
                  <details class="vh-script">
                    <summary>Read the script</summary>
                    <div class="vh-script-body" set:html={v.script.split('\n\n').map(p => `<p>${p}</p>`).join('')} />
                  </details>
                )}
              </div>
            </article>
          ))}
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="vh-cta">
      <div class="vh-wrap">
        <h2>Want more calls?</h2>
        <p>Find out where your buyers stop before they dial.</p>
        <a href="/snapshot" class="btn-blue">See Who's Buying in My Market</a>
      </div>
    </section>

  </main>
  <Footer />
</Base>

<script is:inline>
  // Pause all other videos when one starts playing
  document.addEventListener('play', (e) => {
    if (e.target.tagName !== 'VIDEO') return
    document.querySelectorAll('video').forEach(v => { if (v !== e.target) v.pause() })
  }, true)
</script>

<style>
  .vh-wrap { max-width: 1100px; margin: 0 auto; padding: 0 24px; }

  .vh-hero { background: #f5f5f7; padding: 96px 0 64px; text-align: center; }
  .vh-eyebrow {
    display: inline-block; font-size: 14px; font-weight: 600; letter-spacing: 0.08em;
    text-transform: uppercase; color: #0071e3; margin-bottom: 16px;
  }
  .vh-hero h1 {
    font-family: var(--font-d, -apple-system, sans-serif);
    font-size: clamp(34px, 5vw, 56px); line-height: 1.08; letter-spacing: -0.022em;
    color: #1d1d1f; margin: 0 0 20px;
  }
  .vh-sub { max-width: 560px; margin: 0 auto; font-size: 18px; line-height: 1.5; color: #333333; }

  .vh-grid-section { background: #ffffff; padding: 64px 0 80px; }
  .vh-grid {
    display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fill, minmax(200px, 1fr));
    gap: 36px 24px; max-width: 960px; margin: 0 auto;
  }
  .vh-card { display: flex; flex-direction: column; }
  .vh-player { position: relative; aspect-ratio: 9 / 16; background: #1d1d1f; overflow: hidden; border-radius: 8px; box-shadow: rgba(0,0,0,0.22) 3px 5px 30px 0px; }
  .vh-player video { width: 100%; height: 100%; object-fit: cover; display: block; }

  .vh-meta { padding: 16px 4px 0; }
  .vh-meta h3 { font-size: 17px; font-weight: 600; color: #1d1d1f; margin: 0 0 4px; letter-spacing: -0.01em; }
  .vh-file { font-family: var(--font-m, monospace); font-size: 12px; color: #858585; margin: 0; }

  .vh-script { margin-top: 10px; }
  .vh-script summary {
    cursor: pointer; font-size: 14px; font-weight: 500; color: #0066cc; list-style: none;
  }
  .vh-script summary::-webkit-details-marker { display: none; }
  .vh-script summary::after { content: ' +'; }
  .vh-script[open] summary::after { content: ' −'; }
  .vh-script-body { margin-top: 8px; font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.55; color: #333333; }
  .vh-script-body :global(p) { margin: 0 0 10px; }

  .vh-cta { background: #f5f5f7; padding: 80px 0 96px; text-align: center; }
  .vh-cta h2 { font-size: clamp(28px, 4vw, 40px); letter-spacing: -0.02em; color: #1d1d1f; margin: 0 0 12px; }
  .vh-cta p { font-size: 18px; color: #333333; margin: 0 0 28px; }
  .btn-blue {
    display: inline-block; background: #0071e3; color: #ffffff; font-size: 17px; font-weight: 500;
    padding: 14px 32px; border-radius: 980px; text-decoration: none;
  }
  .btn-blue:hover { background: #0066cc; }

  @media (max-width: 640px) {
    .vh-hero { padding: 64px 0 40px; }
    .vh-grid { grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fill, minmax(160px, 1fr)); gap: 24px 16px; }
  }
</style>
